IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `coq-common`
IMPORT `coq-inquest`

-- Coroners Act 2003 (Queensland) -- worked cases for coq-inquest.l4 (ss 27-30, 36, 39, 45, 46, 46A, 50, 51).
--
-- Every assertion is a claim about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. Expected values were written by hand
-- from the deposited text (act-2003-013, in force from 2026-10-29), never copied from the encoding's output. No scenario
-- life raised this Act; the facts are the encoder's, HYPOTHETICAL throughout. Every "considers" and "is satisfied" of
-- a coroner or court is given as a fact.
--
-- A breached DEONTIC cannot be compared with EQUALS on the stable l4, so the s 30(3) rule is asserted through the
-- questions that use CONSIDER ... WHEN FULFILLED, and one breach is #TRACEd with an "-- expect:" line.
--
-- Unofficial version. Based on content from the Queensland Legislation website at 6 November 2026. (c) The State of
-- Queensland, CC BY 4.0. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

GIVEN d IS A NUMBER, m IS A NUMBER, y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`dmy` d m y MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY d m y

§ `ss 27-29 -- must there be an inquest`

-- Argument order: the coroner's view, satisfied no inquest required (police operations), directed or ordered, someone
-- charged, criminal proceedings ended, inquest started, public interest.
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` `a death in custody (not while detained under the Public Health Act 2005)` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the coroner must hold an inquest, considering the death a death in custody (s 27(1)(a)(i))`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` `a death in care, in circumstances that raise issues about the care` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the coroner must hold an inquest, considering the death a death in care that raises issues about the care (s 27(1)(a)(ii))`
-- A pedestrian killed by a car fleeing police: an inquest unless the coroner is satisfied none is required.
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` `a death in the course of or as a result of police operations, not in custody` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the coroner must hold an inquest into a death in police operations, not being satisfied the circumstances do not require one (s 27(1)(a)(iii))`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` `a death in the course of or as a result of police operations, not in custody` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not required: the coroner need not hold an inquest; you may apply for one (s 30)`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` `a death in the course of or as a result of police operations, not in custody` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `may be held: the coroner is satisfied an inquest is in the public interest (s 28(1))`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` `a mining related reportable death` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the coroner must hold an inquest into a mining related reportable death (s 27(1)(a)(iv))`
-- A workplace death that is none of these, where the State Coroner has ordered an inquest on a family's application.
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` `none of these` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the coroner must hold an inquest, as the Attorney-General, the State Coroner or the District Court has directed or ordered one (s 27(1)(b)-(d), (3))`
-- The driver has been charged with dangerous driving causing death: the inquest waits.
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` `none of these` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not yet: someone has been charged with an offence in which whether they caused the death may be in issue; an inquest must not start until those proceedings, and any appeal, end (s 29(2))`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` `a death in custody (not while detained under the Public Health Act 2005)` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `adjourned: someone has been charged with an offence in which whether they caused the death may be in issue; the inquest must be adjourned, and may resume after those proceedings end (s 29(3))`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` `a death in custody (not while detained under the Public Health Act 2005)` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the coroner must hold an inquest, considering the death a death in custody (s 27(1)(a)(i))`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` `none of these` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `may be held: the coroner is satisfied an inquest is in the public interest (s 28(1))`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` `none of these` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not required: the coroner need not hold an inquest; you may apply for one (s 30)`

§ `s 30(3), (9) -- the coroner's deadline on my application`

-- Received 31 August 2026: 6 months on is 28 February 2027. Received 10 January 2027: 10 July 2027.
#ASSERT `Last day for the inquest decision question` (`dmy` 31 8 2026) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2026) EQUALS `dmy` 28 2 2027
#ASSERT `Last day for the inquest decision question` (`dmy` 10 1 2027) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2026) EQUALS `dmy` 10 7 2027
-- The coroner needs until 30 September 2027 to get an expert report.
#ASSERT `Last day for the inquest decision question` (`dmy` 10 1 2027) TRUE (`dmy` 30 9 2027) EQUALS `dmy` 30 9 2027
-- A "longer" period that ends before 6 months is not longer: the 6 months stand.
#ASSERT `Last day for the inquest decision question` (`dmy` 10 1 2027) TRUE (`dmy` 1 6 2027) EQUALS `dmy` 10 7 2027
-- Argument order: received, needs longer, end of longer period, decided, day decided, today.
#ASSERT `Inquest decision in time question` (`dmy` 10 1 2027) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2026) TRUE (`dmy` 10 7 2027) (`dmy` 1 8 2027) EQUALS `yes: the coroner did it within the period the Act allows`
#ASSERT `Inquest decision in time question` (`dmy` 10 1 2027) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2026) TRUE (`dmy` 11 7 2027) (`dmy` 1 8 2027) EQUALS `no: the coroner did it, but after the period the Act allows had ended`
#ASSERT `Inquest decision in time question` (`dmy` 10 1 2027) TRUE (`dmy` 30 9 2027) TRUE (`dmy` 11 7 2027) (`dmy` 1 8 2027) EQUALS `yes: the coroner did it within the period the Act allows`
#ASSERT `Inquest decision in time question` (`dmy` 10 1 2027) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2026) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2026) (`dmy` 10 7 2027) EQUALS `not yet due: the coroner has not done it, but the period has not ended`
#ASSERT `Inquest decision in time question` (`dmy` 10 1 2027) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2026) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2026) (`dmy` 11 7 2027) EQUALS `overdue: the period the Act allows has ended and the coroner has not done it`
#ASSERT `Inquest decision in time question` (`dmy` 10 1 2027) TRUE (`dmy` 30 9 2027) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2026) (`dmy` 30 9 2027) EQUALS `not yet due: the coroner has not done it, but the period has not ended`
#ASSERT `Inquest decision in time question` (`dmy` 10 1 2027) TRUE (`dmy` 30 9 2027) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2026) (`dmy` 1 10 2027) EQUALS `overdue: the period the Act allows has ended and the coroner has not done it`
-- The rule itself: 181 days from 10 January to 10 July 2027.
#ASSERT `decide the application for an inquest and give written reasons` `done at` 181 `is in time under` (`s 30(3) -- decide the inquest application within the prescribed period` 181)
#ASSERT NOT (`decide the application for an inquest and give written reasons` `done at` 182 `is in time under` (`s 30(3) -- decide the inquest application within the prescribed period` 181))
#TRACE `s 30(3) -- decide the inquest application within the prescribed period` 181 AT 0 WITH
    (WAITUNTIL 200)
-- expect: BREACH BY `the coroner` BECAUSE "s 30(3): ..."

§ `s 30(4)-(7) -- challenging a refusal of an inquest`

-- Reasons received 1 March 2027; the last day is 15 March.
#ASSERT `Can I challenge the refusal of an inquest question` `the investigating coroner, who is not the State Coroner` (`dmy` 1 3 2027) (`dmy` 15 3 2027) EQUALS `yes: apply to the State Coroner for an order that an inquest be held; you are within the 14 days after receiving the written reasons (s 30(4)(a), (5))`
#ASSERT `Can I challenge the refusal of an inquest question` `the State Coroner, as the investigating coroner` (`dmy` 1 3 2027) (`dmy` 15 3 2027) EQUALS `yes: apply to the District Court for an order that an inquest be held; you are within the 14 days after receiving the written reasons (s 30(4)(b), (5))`
#ASSERT `Can I challenge the refusal of an inquest question` `the State Coroner, refusing my application after the investigating coroner's refusal` (`dmy` 1 3 2027) (`dmy` 15 3 2027) EQUALS `yes: apply to the District Court; you are within the 14 days after receiving the State Coroner's written reasons (s 30(6), (7))`
#ASSERT `Can I challenge the refusal of an inquest question` `the investigating coroner, who is not the State Coroner` (`dmy` 1 3 2027) (`dmy` 16 3 2027) EQUALS `no: the application must be made within 14 days after you receive the written reasons, and that period has ended (s 30(5) or (7))`
#ASSERT `Can I challenge the refusal of an inquest question` `the State Coroner, refusing my application after the investigating coroner's refusal` (`dmy` 1 3 2027) (`dmy` 16 3 2027) EQUALS `no: the application must be made within 14 days after you receive the written reasons, and that period has ended (s 30(5) or (7))`
#ASSERT `Can I challenge the refusal of an inquest question` `the State Coroner, as the investigating coroner` (`dmy` 1 3 2027) (`dmy` 1 3 2027) EQUALS `yes: apply to the District Court for an order that an inquest be held; you are within the 14 days after receiving the written reasons (s 30(4)(b), (5))`
#ASSERT `Last day to challenge the refusal of an inquest question` (`dmy` 1 3 2027) EQUALS `dmy` 15 3 2027
#ASSERT `Last day to challenge the refusal of an inquest question` (`dmy` 24 12 2026) EQUALS `dmy` 7 1 2027

§ `s 36 -- taking part in the inquest`

#ASSERT `Can I take part in the inquest question` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: you may appear, examine and cross-examine witnesses, and make submissions, and may be represented by a lawyer (s 36(1)(c), (4))`
#ASSERT `Can I take part in the inquest question` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `in part: you may appear and be represented by a lawyer, but may examine witnesses only with the court's leave and make submissions only about matters the coroner may comment on (s 36(2), (3), (4))`
#ASSERT `Can I take part in the inquest question` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `in part, with leave: you may appear, examine witnesses (with the leave you have) and make submissions about matters the coroner may comment on, and be represented by a lawyer (s 36(2), (3), (4))`
#ASSERT `Can I take part in the inquest question` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 36(1) gives the right only to those it lists; the Coroners Court has not considered you to have a sufficient interest, and the Act does not say whether you may take part otherwise`

§ `s 39 -- incriminating evidence and a later claim`

-- The site supervisor, made to answer, admits removing a guard rail.
#ASSERT `Can the witness's evidence be used against them question` TRUE `the evidence the witness was required to give` `a civil claim (for example for compensation or damages)` EQUALS `no: the evidence the witness was required to give is not admissible against the witness in any other proceeding (s 39(3))`
#ASSERT `Can the witness's evidence be used against them question` TRUE `the evidence the witness was required to give` `a criminal proceeding other than for perjury` EQUALS `no: the evidence the witness was required to give is not admissible against the witness in any other proceeding (s 39(3))`
#ASSERT `Can the witness's evidence be used against them question` TRUE `the evidence the witness was required to give` `a proceeding for perjury` EQUALS `yes: s 39(3) excepts a proceeding for perjury`
-- The maintenance log found because of the admission.
#ASSERT `Can the witness's evidence be used against them question` TRUE `derivative evidence (anything found as a result of that evidence)` `a criminal proceeding other than for perjury` EQUALS `no: derivative evidence is not admissible against the witness in a criminal proceeding (s 39(4))`
#ASSERT `Can the witness's evidence be used against them question` TRUE `derivative evidence (anything found as a result of that evidence)` `a proceeding for perjury` EQUALS `no: derivative evidence is not admissible against the witness in a criminal proceeding (s 39(4))`
#ASSERT `Can the witness's evidence be used against them question` TRUE `derivative evidence (anything found as a result of that evidence)` `a civil claim (for example for compensation or damages)` EQUALS `not covered: s 39 does not bar derivative evidence in a civil proceeding, and the Act says nothing more`
-- Evidence given freely, without refusing: s 39 does not apply.
#ASSERT `Can the witness's evidence be used against them question` FALSE `the evidence the witness was required to give` `a civil claim (for example for compensation or damages)` EQUALS `s 39 gives no protection on these facts: it applies only where the witness refused to give oral evidence because it would tend to incriminate them and the coroner required it`

§ `ss 45(4), 46(2), 46A -- the findings and comments`

-- Argument order: stopped under s 12(2), family member accepting documents, inquest held, appeared with sufficient interest.
#ASSERT `Must I be given the findings question` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no findings need be made: the coroner stopped investigating under s 12(2) (s 45(3)(b))`
#ASSERT `Must I be given the findings question` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: you must be given a written copy of the findings and of any comments (ss 45(4), 46(2))`
#ASSERT `Must I be given the findings question` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: you must be given a written copy of the findings (s 45(4)); comments are made only on deaths investigated at an inquest (s 46(1))`
-- The deceased's employer, which appeared at the inquest.
#ASSERT `Must I be given the findings question` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: you must be given a written copy of the findings and of any comments (ss 45(4), 46(2))`
-- A sibling who did not appear, the spouse having agreed to accept the documents.
#ASSERT `Must I be given the findings question` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `ss 45(4) and 46(2) do not require that you be given a copy: they name the family member who agreed to accept it for the family, and those who appeared at an inquest with a sufficient interest`
#ASSERT `Must I be given the findings question` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `ss 45(4) and 46(2) do not require that you be given a copy: they name the family member who agreed to accept it for the family, and those who appeared at an inquest with a sufficient interest`
-- Publication. Argument order: inquest held, ordered otherwise, public interest, family consulted.
#ASSERT `Will the findings be published question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the findings, and any comments, must be published on the State Coroner's website (s 46A(1))`
#ASSERT `Will the findings be published question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the coroner has ordered that the inquest findings not be published (s 46A(1))`
#ASSERT `Will the findings be published question` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `possibly: without an inquest the coroner may direct publication, considering it in the public interest and having consulted a family member as far as practicable (s 46A(2))`
#ASSERT `Will the findings be published question` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: without an inquest the coroner may direct publication only if it is in the public interest and a family member has been consulted as far as practicable (s 46A(2))`
#ASSERT `Will the findings be published question` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: without an inquest the coroner may direct publication only if it is in the public interest and a family member has been consulted as far as practicable (s 46A(2))`

§ `ss 45(5), 46(3), 51(2) -- the findings in a claim`

#ASSERT `Can the findings help my claim question` `as evidence in a court or tribunal of a fact the findings or comments state` EQUALS `no: the record of the findings and comments is not evidence in any court or tribunal of any fact asserted in it (s 51(2)); and they must not state that anyone is or may be guilty of an offence or civilly liable (ss 45(5), 46(3))`
#ASSERT `Can the findings help my claim question` `to show in a court or tribunal only that the findings or comments were made` EQUALS `not covered: s 51(2) bars the record only as evidence of the facts asserted in it; the findings still must not state that anyone is or may be guilty or civilly liable (ss 45(5), 46(3))`

§ `s 50 -- setting aside a finding`

#ASSERT `Can I have the finding set aside question` FALSE `new evidence casts doubt on the finding` FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 50 is for findings at an inquest; without an inquest the Act gives no application, though the State Coroner or the coroner may reopen the investigation on their own initiative (s 50B)`
-- A later engineering report casts doubt on the finding that the death was accidental.
#ASSERT `Can I have the finding set aside question` TRUE `new evidence casts doubt on the finding` FALSE EQUALS `yes: you may apply to the State Coroner or the District Court; both may set aside a finding on your ground (s 50(1), (4), (5)); s 50 sets no time limit`
#ASSERT `Can I have the finding set aside question` TRUE `the finding was not correctly recorded` FALSE EQUALS `yes: you may apply to the State Coroner or the District Court; both may set aside a finding on your ground (s 50(1), (4), (5)); s 50 sets no time limit`
#ASSERT `Can I have the finding set aside question` TRUE `there was no evidence to support the finding` FALSE EQUALS `yes, to the District Court: only the District Court may set aside a finding on your ground (s 50(5)(c), (d)); s 50 sets no time limit`
#ASSERT `Can I have the finding set aside question` TRUE `the finding could not reasonably be supported by the evidence` FALSE EQUALS `yes, to the District Court: only the District Court may set aside a finding on your ground (s 50(5)(c), (d)); s 50 sets no time limit`
#ASSERT `Can I have the finding set aside question` TRUE `new evidence casts doubt on the finding` TRUE EQUALS `yes, to the District Court only: the District Court has refused on the same or substantially the same grounds, so you may not apply to the State Coroner (s 50(3)); s 50 sets no time limit`
